{
  "slug": "artist:a-guy-called-gerald",
  "name": "A Guy Called Gerald",
  "editorial_article": "Manchester-born Gerald Simpson launched his career as a founding member of 808 State, contributing to their 1988 debut LP Newbuild and the single 'Pacific State'. Shortly after leaving the group, he defined the UK's acid house movement with the 1988 single 'Voodoo Ray', which peaked at number 12 on the UK Singles Chart. By 1991, he established Juice Box Records in West London, using the independent label to release foundational jungle and drum and bass works like his 1995 album Black Secret Technology. His early influences stemmed from his Jamaican roots, including his father's reggae collection and the sound system culture of Moss Side, which he later merged with the electronic sounds of Chicago house and Detroit techno. Throughout his career, he has collaborated with artists such as Goldie, Finley Quaye, and Herbie Hancock, while maintaining an experimental approach that led to live laptop performances for the 2013 project Silent Sound Spread Spectrum.",
